      {
        "type": "text",
        "content": "District 9 revolutionized alien invasion narratives by setting its story in Johannesburg, using apartheid allegory to deliver gut-punching social commentary. Director Neill Blomkamp's documentary-style approach grounds the sci-fi elements in brutal realism, while Sharlto Copley's transformation from bureaucratic stooge Wikus van der Merwe into a desperate fugitive creates visceral horror. The film's innovative mix of found footage, news clips, and traditional cinematography influenced later sci-fi like <a href=\"/movie/329865\" class=\"movie-title\" data-tmdb-id=\"329865\">Arrival</a> (2016) and <a href=\"/movie/7191\" class=\"movie-title\" data-tmdb-id=\"7191\">Cloverfield</a> (2008), while its political metaphors echo through films like <a href=\"/movie/68724\" class=\"movie-title\" data-tmdb-id=\"68724\">Elysium</a> (2013) and <a href=\"/movie/9693\" class=\"movie-title\" data-tmdb-id=\"9693\">Children of Men</a> (2006)."
      },
